/* SPEAKING (x2 bank) — Listen and Repeat (rz001-rz024) + Take an Interview (iz001-iz028) */
(function () {
CONTENT.rep.push(
  { id: "rz001", diff: 2, g: "W", ctx: "You are a student guide leading prospective students around campus. Listen to the tour coordinator and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Welcome, everyone, and thanks for coming this morning.",
    "The tour lasts about an hour and a half.",
    "Our first stop is the science building, just behind the fountain.",
    "Please stay together, and tell me if anyone needs a short break.",
    "The new sports hall opened last year and is free for all students.",
    "Those of you who are interested in engineering can visit the workshops after lunch.",
    "At the end of the tour, we'll answer your questions over coffee, and you can also talk to current students."] },
  { id: "rz002", diff: 2, g: "M", ctx: "You are volunteering at the information desk of a busy train station. Listen to your supervisor and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "Good afternoon, can I help you with anything?",
    "The next train to the airport leaves at four.",
    "Platform six is down the stairs and to your left.",
    "Tickets can be bought at the machines or at this desk.",
    "Because of construction work, some trains are running about fifteen minutes late today.",
    "If you're traveling with a large suitcase, I'd recommend using the elevator at the end of the hall.",
    "Passengers who bought their tickets online can show them on their phones, but they must be ready to scan them before boarding."] },
  { id: "rz003", diff: 2, g: "W", ctx: "You are a new assistant at a campus pharmacy. Listen to the pharmacist and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Please wait here until your name is called.",
    "Most prescriptions can be prepared in about twenty minutes.",
    "This medicine should be taken twice a day, preferably with food.",
    "Always check the name and the date of birth on the label.",
    "If a customer seems confused about the instructions, please explain them again slowly and politely.",
    "Students can get certain medicines at a reduced price, as long as they show their student card.",
    "For safety reasons, we never give out certain medicines without a prescription, even if the customer says they have taken them before."] },
  { id: "rz004", diff: 2, g: "M", ctx: "You are helping to launch a bike-sharing scheme on campus. Listen to the project manager and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "The bikes are parked outside the main library.",
    "You unlock them with the app on your phone.",
    "The first thirty minutes of every ride are completely free.",
    "Please don't leave a bike on the pavement or in front of a door.",
    "If you notice a problem with the brakes, please report it in the app immediately.",
    "Students who return a bike to the wrong station will be charged a small extra fee.",
    "During the exam period, we'll add two hundred more bikes, so you shouldn't have to wait long at the busiest stations."] },
  { id: "rz005", diff: 2, g: "W", ctx: "You are starting a summer job at a hotel. Listen to the front desk manager and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Breakfast is served from seven to ten.",
    "Checkout time is eleven o'clock in the morning.",
    "If guests arrive early, offer to look after their luggage.",
    "Please ask every guest for a name and a room number.",
    "Rooms with a sea view cost more, so tell guests the price before they book.",
    "When a guest makes a complaint, listen carefully, apologize politely, and then let me know right away.",
    "The hotel is fully booked this weekend because of the music festival, so we'll need extra help at the front desk."] },
  { id: "rz006", diff: 3, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a safety briefing before working in a chemistry lab. Listen to the technician and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Please read through the safety rules before you begin.",
    "The emergency shower is located just beside the main door.",
    "Solvents must never be poured down the sink, whatever the quantity.",
    "If the fire alarm goes off, leave everything where it is and walk to the courtyard.",
    "Any broken glassware should be placed in the yellow container rather than in the ordinary waste bin.",
    "Students working with volatile substances are required to use the fume cupboard, and the glass panel must stay below the marked line.",
    "Should you notice an unfamiliar smell or a leak of any kind, stop immediately and warn the people around you before informing a technician."] },
  { id: "rz007", diff: 3, g: "M", ctx: "You are a volunteer at the information desk of a hospital. Listen to the volunteer coordinator and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "Visiting hours begin at two in the afternoon.",
    "Please wear your badge where patients can see it.",
    "Visitors are asked to disinfect their hands at the entrance.",
    "Some wards are restricted, so check the list before you give directions.",
    "If someone looks confused or upset, ask whether they would like you to walk with them.",
    "Patients' medical details are confidential, so never discuss them with anyone who isn't directly involved in their care.",
    "In the event of an emergency, remain calm, direct people toward the nearest exit, and wait for the nursing staff to take charge of the situation."] },
  { id: "rz008", diff: 3, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a psychology lecture about memory. Listen to the professor and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Memory is far less reliable than most people assume.",
    "Every time we recall an event, we partly reconstruct it.",
    "Psychologists distinguish between short-term memory and the much larger long-term store.",
    "Details can be distorted by stress, by leading questions, or simply by time.",
    "Experiments show that people often feel certain about memories that turn out to be inaccurate.",
    "This is why courts are increasingly cautious about relying on a single witness, however confident that person appears to be.",
    "Interestingly, the more often a story is retold, the more convincing it feels to the person telling it, even if some details have quietly changed."] },
  { id: "rz009", diff: 3, g: "M", ctx: "You are attending an environmental science lecture about the oceans. Listen to the professor and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "The oceans absorb a large share of our emissions.",
    "As carbon dioxide dissolves, the seawater becomes gradually more acidic.",
    "This process is particularly damaging to animals that build shells or skeletons.",
    "Coral reefs, which support a quarter of marine species, are especially vulnerable to these changes.",
    "Scientists have measured a drop in ocean pH of about zero point one since the industrial revolution.",
    "Even if emissions stopped tomorrow, it would take thousands of years for the chemistry of the oceans to return to normal.",
    "That is why many researchers argue that cutting emissions is necessary but not sufficient, and that fragile ecosystems also need protection from pollution and overfishing."] },
  { id: "rz010", diff: 3, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a training session for new employees at a consulting firm. Listen to the compliance officer and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Protecting client information is everyone's responsibility in this firm.",
    "Documents containing client names must never be left on printers.",
    "Files may only be shared through the approved platform, never by personal email.",
    "If you lose a laptop or a phone, you must inform the security team within one hour.",
    "Employees who work remotely should avoid public networks, since these can be intercepted far more easily than people imagine.",
    "Any suspected breach has to be reported at once, even if you're not sure that anything has actually been compromised.",
    "Failure to follow these procedures can lead to heavy fines for the company and, in serious cases, to disciplinary action against the individuals responsible."] },
  { id: "rz011", diff: 3, g: "M", ctx: "You are a junior member of an audit team. Listen to the senior auditor and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "We'll meet at the client's offices at eight.",
    "Please bring your laptop, your badge and a copy of the schedule.",
    "Every document we receive must be logged in the shared folder straight away.",
    "Don't accept an explanation from the finance team without asking for some supporting evidence.",
    "Our job is to judge whether the accounts give a true and fair view of the company's position.",
    "If you come across a transaction that looks unusual, note it down and tell me before drawing any conclusions.",
    "Remember that we are independent: even when the client is friendly and helpful, our opinion must rest entirely on the evidence rather than on personal relationships."] },
  { id: "rz012", diff: 3, g: "W", ctx: "You are a new volunteer at the campus radio station. Listen to the station manager and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "You'll be live on air in about five minutes.",
    "Keep the microphone about a hand's width from your mouth.",
    "If you make a mistake, simply carry on and don't apologize repeatedly.",
    "Our listeners are mostly students, so keep the tone relaxed and the segments short.",
    "The red light above the door means we're broadcasting, so please never enter the studio while it's on.",
    "Before each interview, prepare three questions, and make sure your guest knows roughly how long the conversation will last.",
    "Since we rely on volunteers, please arrive on time, and if you can't make your slot, find someone to replace you at least a day beforehand."] },
  { id: "rz013", diff: 3, g: "M", ctx: "You are a guide at an archaeological site that is open to the public. Listen to the site manager and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "Please stay on the marked paths at all times.",
    "Most of what you see here is the original stonework.",
    "Excavations began in the nineteen fifties and are still continuing today.",
    "Please don't climb on the walls, even though they may look sturdy enough.",
    "Visitors often ask why the mosaics are covered, and the reason is that sunlight gradually fades the colors.",
    "Archaeologists believe that the building was a market hall, although some of them argue that it served as a temple.",
    "Because the ground is uneven and the weather changes quickly, we advise sturdy shoes and a waterproof jacket, especially for the afternoon tour of the terraces."] },
  { id: "rz014", diff: 3, g: "W", ctx: "You are at a pre-departure session for students going on exchange. Listen to the study abroad advisor and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Please check that your passport is still valid.",
    "Some universities ask for proof of health insurance on arrival.",
    "You should also register with the consulate of your own country.",
    "Make sure your courses abroad are approved before you leave, not after.",
    "Most students find that their budget stretches less far than expected during the first month.",
    "If you run into difficulties with accommodation, contact the host university's international office before you consider moving out.",
    "Finally, remember that homesickness is perfectly normal, and the students who settle in fastest are usually those who accept invitations, even when they feel tired."] },
  { id: "rz015", diff: 3, g: "M", ctx: "You are chairing a student council meeting for the first time. Listen to the outgoing president and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "Let's begin with the minutes of the last meeting.",
    "Each item on the agenda has a fixed time limit.",
    "Motions need a second before the council can discuss them or vote.",
    "Anyone wishing to speak should raise a hand and wait to be called on.",
    "If a discussion drifts away from the topic, it's your job to bring everyone politely back to it.",
    "Votes are decided by a simple majority, but in the event of a tie, the chair has the casting vote.",
    "Whatever your opinion may be, try to remain neutral while you chair, because the council will only respect your decisions if everyone feels they were heard."] },
  { id: "rz016", diff: 3, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a lecture on behavioral economics. Listen to the professor and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "People don't always make the choices that economists predict.",
    "Small changes in how options are presented can influence behavior.",
    "A nudge steers people toward a better decision without forbidding anything.",
    "Making pension enrollment automatic, for example, dramatically increases the number of people who save.",
    "The explanation is inertia: most people stay with the default, even when changing would cost almost nothing.",
    "Critics point out that nudges can be manipulative, particularly when the person designing them has interests of their own.",
    "Defenders reply that every choice environment is designed by someone anyway, so the real question is whether the designers are transparent about their goals."] },
  { id: "rz017", diff: 4, g: "M", ctx: "You have just joined a start-up incubator as an assistant. Listen to the program director and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "Welcome aboard, and thank you for joining us this week.",
    "Each team here has about six months to prove that its idea can work.",
    "We don't judge founders on their pitch alone, but on how quickly they learn from feedback.",
    "Investors will visit on demo day, so every team must have a working prototype and realistic figures.",
    "Mentors volunteer their time, so arrive prepared and never expect them to solve a problem you haven't tried to tackle yourself.",
    "Funding is rarely the main obstacle at this stage; far more start-ups fail because the founders disagree about direction or run out of energy.",
    "What distinguishes the teams that survive is not a brilliant idea but a stubborn willingness to test assumptions and keep going once the early enthusiasm has faded."] },
  { id: "rz018", diff: 4, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a history lecture about industrialization. Listen to the professor and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "The Industrial Revolution did not happen overnight, despite its name.",
    "Historians now stress how gradual the shift in production really was.",
    "It began with textile mills in Britain, helped by cheap coal and expanding colonial markets.",
    "Yet for several decades, real wages barely improved, even though output per worker was rising considerably.",
    "Many workers experienced industrialization not as progress but as a loss of independence, as skilled trades gave way to factory discipline.",
    "It is therefore misleading to speak of a single revolution, since the benefits arrived late, unevenly, and at a considerable human cost.",
    "What looks in retrospect like an inevitable march toward prosperity was, for those who lived through it, a period of upheaval whose outcome nobody could have confidently predicted."] },
  { id: "rz019", diff: 4, g: "M", ctx: "You are attending a linguistics lecture about how languages change. Listen to the professor and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "Languages are constantly changing, whether speakers notice it or not.",
    "Complaints about linguistic decline are almost as old as writing itself.",
    "Meanings drift over time; the English word nice, for example, once meant foolish.",
    "Sound changes tend to spread gradually through a community, usually starting with younger speakers in urban areas.",
    "Contact between languages is another driver, as the many French words in English since the Norman conquest show.",
    "Linguists therefore avoid describing change as corruption, since every form that now seems correct was once an innovation that purists resisted.",
    "What prescriptive grammarians condemn as a mistake today may be tomorrow's standard usage, which is why describing how people actually speak is more illuminating than legislating how they should."] },
  { id: "rz020", diff: 4, g: "W", ctx: "You are an intern visiting a central bank as part of a university program. Listen to the guide and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "This floor houses the department responsible for supervising commercial banks.",
    "Our central mandate is to keep inflation close to two percent.",
    "When prices rise too quickly, the governing council may decide to raise interest rates.",
    "Such decisions are taken collectively, after weeks of analysis, and announced at a fixed hour.",
    "Because a single remark from the governor can move markets, officials choose their words with extraordinary care.",
    "Independence from the government is considered essential, because elected politicians may be tempted to favor short-term growth over lasting price stability.",
    "Critics counter that this independence gives unelected officials enormous power, whereas supporters maintain that credibility depends precisely on the ability to resist political pressure when it is most uncomfortable."] },
  { id: "rz021", diff: 4, g: "M", ctx: "You are a graduate assistant attending a research ethics training. Listen to the committee chair and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "No study involving human participants may begin without formal approval.",
    "Participants must be told plainly what the research is for and what it involves.",
    "Consent has to be voluntary, which means that nobody should feel pressured into taking part.",
    "Researchers are expected to protect participants' identities, even when the data appear harmless at first sight.",
    "If an experiment turns out to be more distressing than anticipated, it must be paused while the committee reviews the procedure.",
    "Deception is permissible only when no alternative design exists, and participants must be fully informed about it as soon as the session has ended.",
    "Ultimately, ethics approval is not a bureaucratic hurdle but a structured chance to anticipate harms that enthusiastic researchers are often poorly placed to notice themselves."] },
  { id: "rz022", diff: 4, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a philosophy lecture on the ethics of artificial intelligence. Listen to the professor and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Who is responsible when an algorithm makes a harmful decision?",
    "Traditional ethics assumes that moral agents can explain and justify their actions.",
    "Machine learning systems complicate this picture, since their designers often cannot explain individual outputs.",
    "One response is to demand transparency, although fully interpretable models are frequently less accurate than opaque ones.",
    "Another is to insist that a human being remain accountable, which risks turning that person into a symbolic scapegoat.",
    "Some philosophers ask whether responsibility can be shared across developers, regulators, and users without being diluted until nobody is answerable.",
    "The uncomfortable possibility is that our existing moral vocabulary, designed for individual human agents, may simply be inadequate for describing harms that emerge from large, distributed, partly automated systems."] },
  { id: "rz023", diff: 4, g: "M", ctx: "You are a research assistant joining a field trip to a glacier. Listen to the expedition leader and repeat what he says.", s: [
    "We leave the station at six, so breakfast is at five.",
    "Conditions on the ice can deteriorate within minutes, so nobody walks alone.",
    "Everyone must be roped together whenever we cross an area where crevasses may be hidden under snow.",
    "The stakes we planted last spring will show us how much the glacier has moved and thinned since then.",
    "If the visibility drops, we stop immediately, because it is far safer to wait than to guess where the edge is.",
    "Although the data may seem modest, records like these, kept over several decades, are what allow climatologists to separate natural variability from long-term warming.",
    "I know the cold, the early starts and the heavy equipment will test your patience, but very few people ever get the chance to measure a glacier firsthand."] },
  { id: "rz024", diff: 4, g: "W", ctx: "You are attending a seminar on negotiation skills. Listen to the instructor and repeat what she says.", s: [
    "Many people assume that negotiation is simply about winning.",
    "Experienced negotiators begin by trying to understand what the other side really needs.",
    "Positions are what people say they want; interests are the reasons that lie behind those demands.",
    "When two parties focus on interests rather than positions, they often discover solutions that neither of them had imagined.",
    "It also helps to prepare your alternative in advance, because your willingness to walk away determines how much leverage you actually have.",
    "Be careful with first offers: an anchor set too high can destroy trust, while one set too low leaves value on the table.",
    "Above all, remember that a negotiation that leaves the other party feeling cheated rarely ends at the signature, because resentment resurfaces later in poor cooperation and in demands to renegotiate."] }
);

CONTENT.int.push(
  { id: "iz001", diff: 2, g: "W", topic: "Pets and animals", ctx: "You are participating in a survey about people's relationships with animals. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Do you have a pet, or have you ever had one? Please tell me about it.",
    "What responsibilities come with owning a pet?",
    "Some people say that pets are good for people's mental health. Do you agree?",
    "Should wild animals be kept in zoos? Why or why not?"],
    m: [
      "We had a dog when I was a teenager, a golden retriever called Max. My parents got him when I was twelve, and he became part of the family very quickly. He was really clumsy, and he used to steal socks from the laundry basket, which made everyone laugh. Every morning before school, I took him for a short walk, and in the evening he waited by the door for me. When he passed away, we were all very sad, and I think that's when I realized how attached you can become to an animal. I'd love to have another dog one day, but I'd need a bigger apartment first.",
      "The first thing is time. A pet needs daily attention, whether it's walking a dog, cleaning a litter box or just playing with it. You can't simply leave it alone for a long weekend, so you have to find someone to look after it when you travel. Then there's money, because food, vaccines and vet bills can add up quickly, and an unexpected illness can cost hundreds of euros. Finally, you need to be ready to commit for ten or fifteen years. I know people who adopted a puppy on impulse and then gave it away after a few months, which is really unfair to the animal.",
      "Yes, I think so, at least from what I've seen. My aunt lives alone, and since she adopted a cat, she's much more cheerful. She talks to him, she has a routine, and she gets up in the morning because he needs feeding. Research also suggests that stroking an animal lowers stress, and dog owners tend to walk more, which is good for the mind as well as the body. Of course, a pet isn't a cure for serious problems like depression, and it can add stress if you have no time or money. But for many people, an animal brings comfort and a sense of purpose.",
      "I think it depends on the zoo. The old ones, with tiny cages and bored animals, should close, because that's clearly cruel. But modern zoos can play a positive role. For example, some of them run breeding programs that have saved species from extinction, like the Arabian oryx, and they teach children about conservation, which is hard to do through a screen. The problem is that not every animal can live happily in captivity, and elephants or whales are good examples. So my position is that zoos should keep only the species they can look after properly, and they should focus on protecting animals rather than entertaining visitors."] },
  { id: "iz002", diff: 2, g: "M", topic: "Gifts and celebrations", ctx: "You are taking part in a study about how young adults celebrate special occasions. An interviewer will ask you some questions.", q: [
    "How do you usually celebrate your birthday?",
    "What is the best gift you have ever received, and why was it special?",
    "Some people think that spending a lot of money on gifts is unnecessary. What is your opinion?",
    "Do you think big celebrations, like weddings, are worth the cost? Why or why not?"],
    m: [
      "Usually I keep it simple. I invite five or six close friends to a restaurant or to my place, and we have dinner together. I'm not really a fan of big parties, because I'd spend the whole evening worrying about whether everyone is having a good time. My mother always calls in the morning and sings, which is a bit embarrassing but also quite touching. And last year, my friends organized a surprise breakfast before my first class. I had no idea, and I was so happy that I almost cried. For me, it's the people who matter, not the size of the celebration.",
      "The best gift I've ever received was a notebook from my grandfather when I was sixteen. It wasn't expensive, but he had written a short message on the first page, saying that he was proud of me and that I should write down my ideas. I've kept it ever since, and now it's full of lists, plans and half-finished stories. What made it special was that he had thought about me personally. It wasn't just a random item from a shop. He passed away two years ago, so the notebook has become even more precious to me now.",
      "I agree, mostly. A gift is supposed to show that you care, and that doesn't depend on the price. A handwritten card, or a small present that matches the person's interests, is often more meaningful than something expensive. I've also noticed that people sometimes feel uncomfortable when they receive a very costly gift, because they feel they have to give something equally valuable in return. That said, for special occasions like a wedding or a big anniversary, it's normal to spend more. So I'd say the best rule is to spend what you can afford, and to put real thought into it.",
      "It's a difficult question. A wedding is a once-in-a-lifetime event for most people, so I understand why they want it to be memorable. Being surrounded by family and friends is something you'll cherish forever. But I've also seen couples start their married life with large debts after an extravagant party, and that seems unwise. A nice ceremony with a modest budget can be just as moving. In my case, I'd rather spend the money on a deposit for an apartment, or on a long honeymoon. If the guests have a good time and the couple is happy, the size of the budget doesn't change much."] },
  { id: "iz003", diff: 2, g: "W", topic: "Fashion and self-expression", ctx: "You are participating in a survey about clothing and personal style. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "How would you describe your personal style, and how has it changed over the years?",
    "How much attention do you pay to fashion trends?",
    "Some schools require students to wear a uniform. Do you think this is a good idea?",
    "Do you think the way people dress affects how others judge them? Please explain."],
    m: [
      "I'd describe my style as simple and practical. I mostly wear jeans, plain shirts and sneakers, mainly because I want to feel comfortable all day. When I was a teenager, I paid much more attention to brands, and I remember begging my parents to buy me an expensive jacket that everybody at school had. Looking back, it seems silly. Now I prefer a few good-quality items that I can combine in different ways. I also like buying second-hand clothes sometimes, because you find unusual pieces for very little money. So my style has become more personal and less about what other people think.",
      "Not very much, to be honest. I notice what's popular when I walk around the city or scroll through social media, but I don't feel the need to follow every trend. Trends change so quickly that it would be expensive and exhausting to keep up. I'd rather buy something I really like and wear it for years. That said, I sometimes pick up a small idea from a friend or a shop window, like a new color or a different type of jacket. So I'm influenced a little, but I always ask myself whether it suits me before I buy anything.",
      "I see advantages on both sides. A uniform removes the pressure of competing with classmates over brands, so students from poorer families feel less embarrassed. It also saves time in the morning and creates a sense of belonging to the school. On the other hand, clothes are one way for teenagers to express their personality, and a strict uniform can feel like a limit on their freedom. If I had to decide, I'd choose a simple dress code rather than a full uniform, for example plain colors and no logos. That way students keep some choice, but social differences stay less visible.",
      "Unfortunately, yes. People form a first impression within seconds, and clothes are one of the first things they notice. For example, in a job interview, a candidate in a neat jacket usually seems more serious than someone in a worn T-shirt, even if they're equally qualified. I don't think it's fair, because clothes say very little about someone's real abilities or character. But it's how human psychology works. So I try to adapt to the situation: smart clothes when I want to make a good impression, and comfortable ones the rest of the time. It's a bit like using a different register of language depending on the audience."] },
  { id: "iz004", diff: 2, g: "M", topic: "Photography and memories", ctx: "You are taking part in a study about how people record and remember their lives. An interviewer will ask you some questions.", q: [
    "Do you like taking photos? What do you usually photograph?",
    "Tell me about a photograph that is important to you.",
    "Some people say we take so many pictures on our phones that we forget to enjoy the moment. Do you agree?",
    "Do you think photographs are a reliable way of remembering the past? Why or why not?"],
    m: [
      "Yes, I take a lot of photos, mostly with my phone. I like photographing landscapes when I travel, and also small everyday details, like a street corner with nice light or a plate of food that looks good. I rarely take posed pictures, because I find candid ones more natural. At the end of each month I choose my ten favorite pictures and put them in a shared album with my family. It's a small ritual, but it helps me notice good moments that I'd otherwise forget. I'm not a professional at all, I just enjoy it.",
      "There's a photo of my grandparents on their wedding day in nineteen sixty-two, in black and white. My grandmother is laughing at something outside the frame, and my grandfather is looking at her instead of at the camera. It sits on a shelf in my room. I like it because it shows a very ordinary moment, but it tells you so much about their relationship. My grandfather isn't with us anymore, so when I look at it, it feels like he's still there. I've even scanned it, so we won't lose it if the paper gets damaged.",
      "I partly agree. I've been to concerts where half of the audience was filming the whole show, and I thought, they're not really watching it. When you're concentrating on getting the perfect picture, you're less present. On the other hand, a photo can help you relive the moment later, and sharing it with friends is part of the fun. I try to find a compromise: I take two or three pictures at the beginning, then I put my phone away and just enjoy it. That way I have a souvenir, but I don't miss the best part of the experience.",
      "Only up to a point. Photos are great for remembering what a place looked like, but they only show a tiny fraction of reality. They don't capture the sounds, the smells or how you felt. Also, people often choose the pictures that show a happier version of events, which can change our memories. For example, family photos usually show smiling faces, so we may forget the arguments and the boring moments. Research even suggests that people who photograph an object remember fewer details of it afterward. So I use photos to help my memory, but I try not to confuse them with the real thing."] },
  { id: "iz005", diff: 2, g: "W", topic: "Parks and green spaces", ctx: "You are participating in a survey about public spaces in cities. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Is there a park or a green space near where you live? What do you do there?",
    "How do you think parks benefit people who live in large cities?",
    "Some cities want to replace parking spaces with trees and small gardens. Do you support this idea?",
    "If you could design a new public park, what would you include in it?"],
    m: [
      "Yes, there's a big park about ten minutes from my apartment, with a lake and lots of old trees. I go there at least twice a week, usually to jog in the morning, but I also like to sit on a bench with a coffee and a book. On sunny weekends it gets crowded with families having picnics and people playing volleyball, and I love that atmosphere. In autumn, the colors are amazing, and I sometimes go there just to take photos. I honestly don't know what I would do without it, especially after a long day in front of a screen.",
      "I think parks are essential in big cities. First, they give people a place to exercise and relax, which is good for physical and mental health. Studies show that even a short walk among trees reduces stress. Second, trees help to cool the city in summer and clean the air, which matters a lot as temperatures rise. And third, parks are free spaces where people from different backgrounds can meet. A teenager, a retired couple and a family with small kids can all use the same space. In a crowded city, that kind of shared area really improves the quality of life.",
      "Yes, I'd support it, as long as the changes are well planned. Cars take up a huge amount of space in cities, and most of the time they're just parked. Turning some of those spaces into small gardens would make streets more pleasant and reduce summer heat. Of course, some residents and shop owners worry that they'll lose customers or that parking will become a nightmare. I think the answer is to improve public transport at the same time and to offer underground parking nearby. In the long run, people get used to it, and the neighborhood usually becomes more attractive, which is also good for local businesses.",
      "I'd include a large open lawn for sports and picnics, because that's what people use most. Then I'd add a playground for children, with shaded areas so parents can rest. I'd like a small pond with benches around it, and a path for jogging and cycling that's separate from the walking paths. I'd also add a community garden, where residents can grow vegetables, and a café with a terrace. Finally, I'd make sure there's enough lighting and some toilets, because those practical details decide whether a park feels safe and welcoming. Basically, something for every age group and every type of visitor."] },
  { id: "iz006", diff: 2, g: "M", topic: "Festivals and public events", ctx: "You are taking part in a study about local culture and community events. An interviewer will ask you some questions.", q: [
    "Have you ever been to a festival or a big public event? What was it like?",
    "What local traditions or events are important in the place where you grew up?",
    "Some people say that big festivals are mainly about making money for businesses. Do you agree?",
    "Do you think cities should spend public money on festivals and cultural events? Why or why not?"],
    m: [
      "Yes, two summers ago I went to a music festival with five friends. We camped for three days, and honestly the camping was both the best and the worst part. The weather was hot, the showers were far away, and we barely slept, but we saw about twenty bands, including a few that I'd never heard of and now love. The atmosphere was incredible. Thousands of people were singing together, and nobody cared about their phones. I remember dancing in the rain on the last night, completely soaked, and being really happy. I'd definitely do it again, probably with better shoes.",
      "In my hometown, the most important event is the harvest festival in September. There's a parade in the main street, with old tractors, traditional costumes and brass bands. Local farmers set up stalls selling cheese, honey and wine, and in the evening there's a big dinner in the square. When I was a child, I couldn't wait for it, because there were rides and sweets everywhere. Now I appreciate it for different reasons: it brings together people of all generations, and it reminds us that the region's identity is closely linked to agriculture. Even people who left the town usually come back for that weekend.",
      "There's some truth to it. Large festivals attract sponsors, and ticket prices can be very high, so money is clearly a big part of the picture. But I wouldn't say it's the only thing. Many festivals started because communities wanted to celebrate something, and the organizers often work for little pay. Also, visitors spend money in hotels, restaurants and shops, so the local economy benefits. The problem arises when commercial interests take over completely, and the event loses its original character. I think the best festivals are the ones that stay connected to local culture, even if they have to make a profit to survive.",
      "I think it's a good use of money, within limits. Cultural events bring people together, they make a city more attractive to residents and tourists, and they support artists and small businesses. In many cases, the money returns to the city through tourism and taxes. However, the budget must be spent transparently, and the events should be accessible, for instance with free or cheap tickets for locals. If the money goes only to huge shows that most residents can't afford, it's harder to justify. So I'd support public funding, provided that it benefits the whole community and not just a small audience."] },
  { id: "iz007", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Social media habits", ctx: "You have agreed to take part in a study about young adults and social media. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "How much time do you spend on social media in a typical day, and what do you use it for?",
    "Have you ever taken a break from social media? What happened?",
    "Some researchers believe that social media has a negative effect on young people's self-esteem. Do you agree?",
    "Should there be an age limit for using social media? How could it be enforced?"],
    m: [
      "Probably around an hour and a half on a normal day, although I'd rather not check the exact figure on my phone. Most of it is passive scrolling, to be honest: short videos, news accounts and the occasional post from friends. I also use messaging apps all day, which I wouldn't really count as social media, but they take up a lot of time too. I use one professional network to follow companies I'm interested in and to keep in touch with former colleagues. The thing is, I rarely post anything myself. I mostly consume, and I sometimes end up wasting twenty minutes without remembering what I saw.",
      "Yes, I did a month-long break last January, after realizing that I reached for my phone every time I felt bored. The first week was strange. I kept unlocking my phone out of habit and finding nothing to look at. After that, though, I noticed that I was reading more, sleeping better and concentrating for longer. I also called friends instead of just liking their posts, which made our conversations deeper. When I went back, I kept the apps off my home screen and set a daily limit. It didn't turn me into a different person, but it made me far more aware of how much attention these platforms demand.",
      "I think there's solid evidence that heavy use can harm some young people, particularly teenagers, who are constantly comparing themselves with idealized images. Filters and carefully selected photos create unrealistic standards, and likes become a kind of public score. That said, the effect isn't uniform. For people who feel isolated, social media can offer support and a sense of community, for example for those with rare interests or health conditions. So the question is less whether to use it than how. Passive scrolling seems to be the most harmful, while active, meaningful interaction can be positive. In other words, it's about the kind of use, not just the amount.",
      "I do think there should be a minimum age, but I'm skeptical about how effective it can be. Currently, many platforms set the limit at thirteen, but children simply lie about their birth date, and nobody checks. A stricter limit would only work if the platforms were obliged to verify age, perhaps through official identity systems. That raises privacy concerns, because people would have to hand over personal data. A realistic approach might combine a higher age limit, say fifteen or sixteen, with parental controls and school education about online risks. No single measure will be perfect, but together they could make a real difference."] },
  { id: "iz008", diff: 3, g: "M", topic: "Public speaking", ctx: "You are participating in a university study about communication skills. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "How do you feel when you have to speak in front of an audience?",
    "What do you do to prepare for an important presentation?",
    "Some people believe that good speakers are born, not made. Do you agree?",
    "In the age of video and written communication, is public speaking still an important skill? Why?"],
    m: [
      "To be honest, I get quite nervous in the first minute or so. My heart beats faster, my voice sounds higher than usual and I tend to speak too quickly. But after the first couple of sentences, I usually settle down, especially if I can see that people are listening. I remember my first presentation at university: I had memorized everything, and when I lost my place, I completely froze for about ten seconds, which felt like an hour. Since then, I've learned that nobody notices small mistakes as much as the speaker does, and that realization has made me far calmer.",
      "I start by deciding on the one main message I want the audience to remember, and then I build a simple structure around it, usually three main points. I never write the whole speech, because reading from paper sounds dull; I only note keywords. Then I rehearse out loud, ideally in front of a friend, and I time myself, because we tend to underestimate how long we speak. On the day itself, I arrive early to check the room and the equipment. Finally, I take a few slow breaths before I begin, which really helps me stay composed.",
      "I disagree, at least partly. Some people are naturally more confident or charismatic, and that gives them a head start. But most of the skills involved can be learned: structuring a talk, using pauses, making eye contact and telling a story. I've seen very shy classmates become excellent speakers after taking a debating course and practicing every week. What matters most is practice and feedback. The idea that talent is fixed can actually be harmful, because it discourages people from trying. I'd say that natural ability helps at the start, but after a few years, a hardworking speaker will usually outperform a gifted but unprepared one.",
      "Absolutely. Even though so much communication is written or recorded, the moments that decide careers often happen live: a job interview, a meeting with a client, a pitch to investors. In those situations, you have to convince people in real time and respond to questions you didn't expect. Public speaking also trains clarity of thought. If you can't explain your idea in three minutes, you probably haven't understood it well enough. And while a video can be edited a dozen times, a live talk shows who you really are, which is why people tend to trust it more."] },
  { id: "iz009", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Advertising and consumer behavior", ctx: "You are taking part in a marketing research study about how people react to advertising. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Can you think of an advertisement that made an impression on you? What did you like or dislike about it?",
    "Do you think advertising influences your own purchasing decisions?",
    "Some people argue that advertising aimed at children should be banned. What is your view?",
    "How has digital advertising changed the relationship between companies and consumers?"],
    m: [
      "One that comes to mind is an old commercial for a mobile phone company, with a grandmother video-calling her grandson abroad. There was almost no dialogue, just the two of them laughing and some quiet piano music. What I liked was that the product was barely shown; the advertisement was really about being close to people even when you're far away. I found it much more effective than the usual list of technical features. On the other hand, I really dislike ads that interrupt a video every few minutes, especially when they're loud. In that case, I just feel irritated with the brand.",
      "I'd like to say no, but I know that's not realistic. Even when I don't consciously notice an ad, I recognize brands I've seen many times, and that familiarity makes them feel safer. For example, when I'm in a supermarket and there are ten similar products, I tend to pick the one I've seen advertised. I try to counter this by comparing prices and reading reviews, particularly for expensive purchases. So, for big decisions, I think I'm fairly rational. For small everyday items, though, advertising probably has more influence than I'd care to admit, and I try to stay aware of that.",
      "I'm inclined to support strict limits. Young children can't tell the difference between information and persuasion, so they're far more vulnerable than adults. Advertising for sugary snacks or toys can lead them to pester their parents, which creates conflict at home and encourages unhealthy habits. Some countries, like Sweden, already forbid television commercials aimed at children under twelve, and it seems to work. The practical difficulty is the internet, where influencers promote products without always making it clear that they're being paid. So any ban would have to include online content, otherwise companies will simply move there.",
      "It's made the relationship far more personalized, and also more intrusive. Companies can now track what you search for and show you ads tailored to your interests, which is more efficient for them and sometimes more useful for us. But it also means they collect huge amounts of personal data, often without us fully understanding it. On the positive side, small businesses can reach a targeted audience with a modest budget, which wasn't possible in the era of television. And consumers have a voice too: a bad review can spread within hours. So the balance of power has shifted a bit, although the big platforms still hold most of the cards."] },
  { id: "iz010", diff: 3, g: "M", topic: "Remote work", ctx: "You have agreed to participate in a study about changing workplaces. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Have you ever worked or studied remotely? What was your experience?",
    "What are the main advantages of working from home, in your opinion?",
    "Some managers believe that employees are less productive when they work from home. Do you agree?",
    "How might remote work change cities and communities in the next decade?"],
    m: [
      "Yes, during one semester I followed several courses online, and later I did a part-time internship partly from home. The advantage was flexibility: no commute, and I could organize my day around my most productive hours. The drawback was the isolation. I realized how much I'd relied on casual conversations in the corridor to feel part of a team. At home, the boundaries between work and personal life became blurred, and I caught myself answering emails at ten at night. So I ended up setting strict hours and working from a café once a week, just to see other people.",
      "The most obvious one is time. Without a commute, you can gain an hour or two a day, which you can use for sleep, exercise or family. It's also better for the environment, since fewer people travel. Another benefit is autonomy: you can organize your tasks the way you want, and many people find they concentrate better without the interruptions of an open-plan office. And from a company's point of view, remote work allows hiring talented people regardless of where they live. Of course, it doesn't suit every job or every person, but for many roles, those advantages are quite significant.",
      "I think it depends on the nature of the work and on the management. For tasks that require deep concentration, like analysis or writing, many people are actually more productive at home. But for creative collaboration or training newcomers, being in the same room still makes a difference, because ideas flow faster and problems are solved on the spot. I'd also say that managers who equate presence with productivity are measuring the wrong thing. A better approach is to focus on results: agree on clear objectives and judge people on what they deliver, not on where they sit.",
      "I think we'll see a gradual redistribution. City centers might lose some of their office workers, which would hurt cafés and shops that relied on lunchtime customers, so many of them will have to adapt. At the same time, medium-sized towns and rural areas could benefit if people move there for cheaper housing and a better quality of life. Some office buildings may be converted into apartments, which could help the housing shortage. On the other hand, I worry about communities where nobody is around during the day, because local life depends on people being present. So the effects will be mixed, and cities will have to rethink how they use their space."] },
  { id: "iz011", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Moving to a new country", ctx: "You are participating in a research project about international mobility. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "If you had to move to another country for work, which one would you choose and why?",
    "What do you think would be the biggest challenge of settling in a new country?",
    "Some people say that immigrants should adapt fully to the culture of their new country. What is your opinion?",
    "How does living abroad change the way people see their own country?"],
    m: [
      "I'd probably choose Canada, for a few reasons. First, it has strong financial centers like Toronto, and the working culture is known for being friendly and well organized. Second, I speak both English and French, so I'd be able to use both languages, particularly in Montreal. And I've heard that it's very welcoming to newcomers, with clear immigration procedures for skilled workers. I also like the idea of being close to nature: you can work in the city during the week and go hiking or skiing on weekends. It's quite far from my family, but with video calls and holidays, I think I could manage.",
      "I think the hardest part is probably building a social circle. Practical problems, such as finding an apartment or opening a bank account, are stressful, but they can be solved with paperwork and patience. Making real friends is different, because you start from zero. At first, you're surrounded by acquaintances, and the evenings and weekends can feel very empty. Language is another issue, even if you speak it well, since humor, slang and cultural references take years to understand. I'd try to join clubs or sports teams quickly, because shared activities make it much easier to meet people than waiting for invitations.",
      "I think there should be a middle path. Newcomers obviously need to learn the language and respect the laws, because that's essential for working and living together. But asking people to abandon their own traditions seems unrealistic, and honestly, a bit unfair. Cultural diversity can enrich a society, in food, music and ideas. Look at cities like London or New York, where much of the dynamism comes from the mix of backgrounds. So I'd talk about integration rather than assimilation: people take part fully in the life of the country while keeping the parts of their heritage that matter to them.",
      "In my experience, and from what friends tell me, it makes you both more critical and more appreciative. You start to notice things you used to take for granted, like how efficient the public transport is, or how people greet each other in the street. At the same time, you become aware of flaws you'd never noticed, simply because everyone around you accepted them. One of my friends who spent two years in Japan told me that she now sees her own country as noisy and slightly disorganized, but also warmer. So distance gives you perspective, which is probably the most valuable thing about living abroad."] },
  { id: "iz012", diff: 3, g: "M", topic: "Role models", ctx: "You are taking part in a study about the people who influence young adults. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Who is someone you admire, and what do you admire about them?",
    "Do you think it's important for young people to have role models? Why?",
    "Some people argue that celebrities have a responsibility to set a good example. Do you agree?",
    "Is it better to admire someone's achievements or their character? Explain your view."],
    m: [
      "I really admire my former high school coach. He was a volunteer who trained our basketball team three evenings a week for years, even though he had a demanding job in a bank. What impressed me most was his patience. He never shouted when we lost; he asked us what we had learned. He also treated everyone equally, whether you were the best player or someone who barely touched the ball. I think I learned more about discipline and fairness from him than from many classes. When I'm in a team now, I often ask myself what he would do.",
      "Yes, I think they play an important role, particularly during adolescence, when people are building their identity. A role model shows what is possible. If a girl sees a woman running a large company, or a student from a modest background becoming a doctor, it makes those careers feel reachable. Role models also give direction when you're unsure about your future. The risk is choosing someone with the wrong values, or copying them without thinking. That's why it's helpful to have several role models rather than only one, and to admire specific qualities instead of an entire person.",
      "To some extent, yes. When you have millions of followers, your behavior is bound to influence people, especially children. It seems reasonable to expect that public figures don't promote dangerous habits or spread misleading information. At the same time, I think it's unfair to demand perfection. Celebrities are human, they make mistakes, and they didn't choose to be role models; they chose to be singers or athletes. So I'd say they have a responsibility not to cause harm, but not to be saints. Parents and teachers have to do their part too, by helping children think critically about who they look up to.",
      "I'd say character matters more, in the long run. Achievements can be impressive, but they sometimes result from luck, privilege or even questionable behavior. Character, on the other hand, shows in how someone treats others when nobody is watching. I can admire a scientist's discoveries, but if I learn that she mistreated her students, my admiration drops. That said, achievements are easier to see, so they're usually what attracts us in the first place. The ideal, of course, is someone who combines both, and I suspect that the people who last in our memory are the ones who do."] },
  { id: "iz013", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Waste and recycling", ctx: "You have agreed to take part in a survey about consumption and the environment. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "What do you usually do with things you no longer need, such as old clothes or electronics?",
    "Why do you think so many people struggle to reduce the amount of waste they produce?",
    "Some cities charge households according to the amount of waste they throw away. Do you think this is fair?",
    "Will it ever be possible for societies to produce almost no waste? Why or why not?"],
    m: [
      "I try not to throw things away if they still work. Clothes in good condition go to a charity shop or to a friend's younger sibling, and I sell electronics like old phones online. Last year I replaced my laptop and gave the old one to a neighbor's daughter for school, which felt much better than recycling it. For items that are really broken, I take them to the electronics collection point near the supermarket. And when I can, I try to repair things first. I recently fixed a zipper on my backpack by following a video tutorial, which saved me forty euros.",
      "I think it's mostly a question of convenience and habit. Modern life is built around disposable products: coffee cups, packaging, fast fashion. It's often easier and cheaper to buy something new than to repair the old one. Many people also assume that their small contribution doesn't matter, which makes them less motivated. And recycling rules can be confusing, so people put things in the wrong bin without realizing. To really change behavior, we need to make the sustainable option the easiest one, with clear labeling, deposit schemes for bottles, and products designed to last. Otherwise, good intentions alone won't be enough.",
      "I think it's fair in principle, because it follows the polluter-pays idea: people who create more waste contribute more to the cost of dealing with it. It also gives households a clear incentive to sort and reduce. Where I'd be cautious is with the practical effects. Large families, or people living in small apartments with limited storage, might be penalized unfairly, and there is a risk of illegal dumping, as some places have experienced. So I'd support it if it comes with generous recycling services and discounts for low-income households. A fair scheme has to encourage good behavior without punishing those who have no alternatives.",
      "Producing absolutely no waste is probably unrealistic, but getting close is not impossible. A circular economy, where products are designed to be reused, repaired and recycled, could reduce waste dramatically. Some companies already take back their products and reuse the materials, and some cities already recycle or compost most of what they collect. The obstacles are mostly economic and political: it's often cheaper to extract new raw materials than to recycle old ones. If governments tax new resources more heavily and require durable design, the economics would change. So I'm cautiously optimistic, provided there's a serious policy commitment."] },
  { id: "iz014", diff: 3, g: "M", topic: "Stress and pressure", ctx: "You are participating in a university study on student well-being. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "What situations make you feel most stressed, and how do you deal with them?",
    "Do you think a little stress can be useful? Why or why not?",
    "Some universities have introduced mental health days for students. Do you think this is a good idea?",
    "Why do you think so many young people report feeling anxious about their future?"],
    m: [
      "Exams and deadlines are the main ones, especially when several fall in the same week. I get a tight feeling in my chest and I start sleeping badly. What helps me is breaking the workload into small steps and writing a realistic schedule, because most of the anxiety comes from the vague feeling that there's too much to do. I also go for a run, even if I think I don't have time, because afterward I think more clearly. And I try to talk about it with friends, since I've noticed that almost everyone feels the same way, which is oddly comforting.",
      "Yes, I do. There's a well-known idea in psychology that performance improves with moderate pressure, then falls when stress becomes too strong. Before an exam, a bit of nervousness makes me focus and review more carefully. Without any pressure, I tend to postpone everything. The key is the dose and the duration. Short-term stress that pushes you to act is useful; chronic stress that never lets up is harmful, because it affects sleep, health and relationships. So I try to treat the feeling as a signal that something matters, rather than as an enemy, and then I make sure I recover afterward.",
      "I think it's a positive step, as long as it's organized properly. Mental health is just as important as physical health, and nobody questions sick days for the flu. A day off can prevent a small problem from becoming a serious one. However, I'd worry about the day becoming a symbol while the underlying causes remain unchanged: heavy workloads, financial worries, lack of counseling services. So I'd see it as a useful complement, but not a substitute for investing in real support, such as more psychologists on campus and flexible deadlines in exceptional cases. Otherwise, it risks being a gesture rather than a real solution.",
      "I think several factors combine. The job market is less predictable than it used to be, and a degree no longer guarantees stable employment. Housing is expensive, so the milestones that earlier generations reached at twenty-five, like buying a flat, feel out of reach. On top of that, social media constantly shows us other people's successes, which makes us feel behind. Add the climate crisis and political uncertainty, and it's not surprising that young people feel anxious. At the same time, I think we're more willing to talk about it, so part of the increase may simply be that anxiety is more visible."] },
  { id: "iz015", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Goals and motivation", ctx: "You are taking part in a research project about personal goals. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "What is a goal you are currently working toward?",
    "How do you stay motivated when a goal takes a long time to achieve?",
    "Some people say that setting very ambitious goals is better than setting realistic ones. What is your opinion?",
    "Is motivation something that comes from inside a person, or can it be created by the environment?"],
    m: [
      "At the moment, I'm preparing for the TOEFL, because I want to apply to a master's program in finance abroad. My target is a score of a hundred and ten. I've set myself a weekly plan: three speaking sessions, two listening sessions and one full practice test on Saturdays. It's demanding, but having a clear date for the exam helps. I'm also working toward a more personal goal, which is to run a half marathon next spring. Both require regular effort rather than sudden bursts, and I like the idea that progress comes from small, repeated actions.",
      "For me, the key is to track progress visibly. When I can see that I've moved from a certain score to a better one, or from five kilometers to eight, I feel that the effort is paying off. I also divide the long-term goal into milestones, so I can celebrate smaller victories along the way. On days when I really don't feel like working, I tell myself that I only need to do ten minutes. Usually I continue after that. And I remind myself why I started, by keeping a note on my desk with the reasons. It sounds simple, but it works surprisingly well.",
      "I think it's a bit of both. Ambitious goals can push you to do more than you thought possible, and they give a sense of purpose. But if the goal is too far from where you are now, you may feel discouraged after a few failures and give up. Realistic goals build confidence, and confidence feeds ambition. My preference is to set one ambitious long-term target and then break it into realistic short-term steps. That way, you aim high, but you also get regular proof that you're moving in the right direction, which is what keeps people going.",
      "I believe it's both, but the environment is underrated. Some people seem naturally driven, yet even they need conditions that support them. If you're surrounded by people who value effort, if you get feedback and if the goal is meaningful, you're much more likely to stay motivated. I've noticed that I work harder in a library full of focused students than alone at home. Teachers and managers can create that environment by giving autonomy, clear goals and recognition. So I'd say motivation isn't a fixed trait; it's something that grows when the conditions are right."] },
  { id: "iz016", diff: 3, g: "M", topic: "Museums and history", ctx: "You are participating in a study about how people relate to the past. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Do you enjoy visiting museums? Tell me about one you remember well.",
    "Why is it important for people to learn about history?",
    "Some people think museums should return artifacts to the countries where they originally came from. What do you think?",
    "How might technology change the way people experience museums in the future?"],
    m: [
      "Yes, I do, as long as the museum isn't too big. I remember visiting a small museum of industrial history in a former factory. The guide was a retired engineer who had worked there, and he told stories about the machines and the workers. It made the past feel real, much more than reading a textbook. There was a huge steam engine that they still started once a day, and the noise and the smell were unforgettable. I think the best museums are those where you feel a human connection, instead of just seeing objects behind glass.",
      "I think history helps us understand why the present looks the way it does. Borders, institutions, even economic systems are the result of past decisions, and without that knowledge, current events can seem random. It also teaches us humility, because we see how often people were certain about things that later turned out to be wrong. And finally, it helps us recognize patterns. For example, a finance student who knows about the crisis of nineteen twenty-nine or two thousand and eight will be more alert to the warning signs of a bubble. Those who ignore history tend to repeat its mistakes.",
      "It's a complex issue, and I think each case deserves individual consideration. Some objects were clearly taken during colonial rule, under unequal conditions, and many people feel that keeping them is hard to justify. Returning them can be an act of justice and reconciliation. On the other hand, museums argue that they preserve the objects and make them available to millions of visitors. I'd find a compromise acceptable: return the objects where a clear claim exists, and organize loans, joint exhibitions and digital copies so that everyone can still learn from them. But the starting point should be listening to the countries concerned.",
      "I think technology will make museums more interactive. Visitors might use augmented reality to see a ruined building as it looked two thousand years ago, just by pointing their phone at it. Digital reconstructions already let us explore fragile places that are closed to the public. For people who can't travel, virtual tours could offer access to collections worldwide. The challenge is not to let technology overshadow the real objects. Seeing an original painting, with its texture and scale, is a very different experience from looking at a screen. So the best museums will probably use technology to deepen the visit, not to replace it."] },
  { id: "iz017", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Gap years and taking a break", ctx: "You have agreed to take part in a study about young people's life choices. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Would you consider taking a year off between finishing school and starting university, or between your studies and your career? Why?",
    "What activities do you think would be most valuable during a gap year?",
    "Some employers worry about gaps in a resume. Do you think these concerns are justified?",
    "In general, do you think young people today rush too quickly into adult responsibilities?"],
    m: [
      "I've thought about it seriously. After my bachelor's degree, I could have taken a year to work abroad before starting my master's. In the end, I decided against it, mainly for financial reasons and because I felt eager to continue. But I don't think it's a bad idea. A year off can help you clarify what you really want, instead of following the path that everyone expects. If I were unsure about my direction, I would definitely take one. For now, I prefer to study, work in a bank for a couple of years and then see whether a longer break makes sense.",
      "I think the most valuable ones combine work and learning. For example, working abroad in a hostel or on a farm teaches you independence and a language at the same time. Volunteering for a serious organization is also great, because you meet people from other backgrounds and discover problems you only knew from the news. An internship in a field you're curious about can help you test your interests. Traveling is wonderful, but I'd say it's most valuable when there's a project behind it, such as learning a skill or writing a blog. Otherwise it risks being just a long holiday.",
      "Partly, yes. A gap with no explanation can raise questions, because recruiters want to know how you spent your time. But I think the concern is exaggerated when the break was used meaningfully. A candidate who spent a year volunteering, learning a language or working on a personal project might actually stand out, since it shows initiative and maturity. The key is how you talk about it in the interview: you need to explain what you did, and what you learned from it. If a recruiter dismisses someone only because of a gap, that probably tells you something about the company culture.",
      "In many cases, yes. Young people often feel they have to choose a career at eighteen, finish their studies quickly and be financially independent by twenty-five. That's a lot of pressure at an age when you're still discovering who you are. At the same time, economic reality plays a role: with high tuition fees and expensive housing, many students can't afford to slow down. I'd say the problem isn't that they're mature too soon, it's that they have so little room for experiments. A society that allows people to try things and change direction probably gets more motivated and more creative adults."] },
  { id: "iz018", diff: 3, g: "M", topic: "Competition and cooperation", ctx: "You are participating in a study about attitudes toward competition. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Do you consider yourself a competitive person? Please explain.",
    "Can you give an example of a time when competition helped you improve?",
    "Some educators believe that schools should focus on cooperation instead of competition. Do you agree?",
    "In business, is competition always good for consumers? Why or why not?"],
    m: [
      "Yes, I'd say I'm moderately competitive. I like to win, whether it's at table tennis or in a case competition at university, and I notice that I try harder when there is a ranking. But I'm not someone who enjoys defeating others for its own sake. I prefer competing against my own previous results, like trying to run a faster time or get a higher score on a practice test. And in group work, I'm much more cooperative than competitive. I think it's healthy to want to do your best, as long as you stay respectful and don't let the desire to win ruin your friendships.",
      "Yes, definitely. In my last year of high school, a classmate and I were both aiming for the top grade in mathematics. We never said so openly, but each time one of us got a good mark, the other wanted to do better. We started solving extra problems and checking each other's solutions, so we ended up learning much more than we would have alone. In the end, we both got excellent grades, and we became good friends. That's the best kind of competition for me: one where the rivalry pushes you forward, and you respect the other person.",
      "I'd say schools need both. Competition can motivate students and teach them how to handle winning and losing, which are part of real life. But if it dominates everything, it creates anxiety, and weaker students may give up. Cooperation, on the other hand, teaches communication and shared responsibility, and those are skills that employers look for. Group projects where students depend on each other, combined with some friendly contests, seem to me the best mix. What I'd avoid is publicly ranking students all the time, because that turns learning into a comparison instead of a personal journey.",
      "Generally yes, but not always. Competition pushes companies to lower prices, improve quality and innovate, which clearly benefits consumers. Think of how mobile phone and airline prices have fallen over the past twenty years. But there are exceptions. If competition is too aggressive, companies may cut corners on safety or labor conditions. And in some markets, a few giants dominate and the competition is more apparent than real, so prices stay high. That's why regulators exist: to prevent cartels and abuse of dominant positions. So competition is a powerful tool for consumers, but it needs rules to work properly."] },
  { id: "iz019", diff: 3, g: "W", topic: "Creativity", ctx: "You are taking part in a study about creativity at school and at work. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Do you think of yourself as a creative person? In what ways?",
    "Can creativity be taught, or is it something people are born with?",
    "Some people say that technology, such as AI tools, is making people less creative. Do you agree?",
    "Why do you think companies are increasingly looking for creative thinkers, even in fields like finance?"],
    m: [
      "I wouldn't call myself artistic, but I think I'm creative in a practical way. I enjoy finding unusual solutions to problems, like designing a better filing system for my notes or inventing a new recipe from the leftovers in the fridge. In group projects, I'm often the person who suggests a different angle, for example approaching a case study from the customer's point of view instead of the accountant's. I also write short stories occasionally, just for myself. So creativity, for me, isn't only about painting or music; it's about looking at a situation and asking whether it could be done differently.",
      "I believe it can be developed. Some people may have a natural inclination, but creativity involves habits that anyone can practice: asking unusual questions, combining ideas from different fields, tolerating mistakes and taking time to think. Many designers and writers describe their process as disciplined work, not sudden inspiration. Schools can help by giving open-ended assignments instead of only asking for one correct answer. I think it also requires a safe environment, because people won't propose original ideas if they're afraid of being laughed at. So it's partly personality, but mostly practice and context, and in my experience curiosity feeds it more than talent does.",
      "I see both sides. On the one hand, if people rely on AI to produce a first draft of everything, they might stop exercising their imagination, just as we've stopped memorizing phone numbers. The results can also look similar, because the tools tend to produce the most likely answer. On the other hand, these tools can serve as a springboard: they offer ten possibilities in a minute, and a creative person can select, combine and improve them. So I think the outcome depends on how we use the tools. If we treat them as a partner, they can enhance creativity; if we use them as a shortcut, they will weaken it.",
      "Because routine tasks are increasingly automated, and what remains are problems that don't have an obvious answer. In finance, for instance, software can calculate a valuation in seconds, but deciding which assumptions are realistic, or how to structure an unusual deal, requires imagination as well as technical knowledge. Creative thinkers also spot opportunities that others overlook, such as a new financial product or a better way to serve clients. Companies know that competitors can copy technology fairly quickly, but they can't easily copy a culture where people think differently. That's why creativity has become a real competitive advantage."] },
  { id: "iz020", diff: 4, g: "M", topic: "Online privacy", ctx: "You are participating in a study about digital life and personal data. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "How careful are you about the personal information you share online?",
    "Many people accept terms and conditions without reading them. Why do you think that happens?",
    "Some argue that privacy is no longer realistic in the digital age, and that people should simply accept it. How would you respond?",
    "Where should the line be drawn between a company's right to use data and an individual's right to privacy?"],
    m: [
      "I'd say I'm moderately careful, though probably less than I should be. I use a password manager, I have two-step verification on my main accounts and I've made my social profiles private. I'm also selective about what I post, and I never share my address or travel plans in advance. Where I'm less rigorous is with apps. I often grant permissions without checking what the app actually needs, simply because I want to use it right away. Last year I discovered that a simple flashlight app had access to my contacts, and I deleted it immediately. It was a useful reminder that convenience is often what gets us.",
      "Fundamentally, because the cost of reading them is immediate and the cost of ignoring them is invisible. These documents are long, written in dense legal language, and presented at the very moment when you want to use the service. Nobody has the time to read forty pages for a free app. There's also a sense of powerlessness: since it's usually a take-it-or-leave-it offer, people conclude that reading wouldn't change anything. Researchers have estimated that reading every policy we agree to would take weeks every year. So the system more or less relies on our inattention, which is arguably a flaw in the design, not in the users.",
      "I'd push back on that. It's true that total privacy is impossible, but it doesn't follow that we should give up. We don't say that since crime exists, we should abandon laws. Privacy protects autonomy: if people know they're being watched, they behave differently and become more conformist. It also protects vulnerable people, for example someone whose medical data could be used against them by an insurer or an employer. Regulations like the European data protection rules show that meaningful limits are possible. So I'd say that privacy is no longer a given, it has become something we have to defend deliberately.",
      "I think the principle should be that data belongs to the individual, and companies may use it only for what the person has clearly agreed to, and for a defined period. Using purchase history to improve a service seems acceptable; selling sensitive information, like health data or political views, to third parties without explicit consent does not. I'd also want transparency, so people can see what's stored and delete it. The difficulty is enforcement, because the companies are large and the technology evolves faster than the law. That's why independent regulators with real power to impose fines are, in my opinion, essential."] },
  { id: "iz021", diff: 4, g: "W", topic: "Cashless society", ctx: "You have agreed to take part in a study about the future of payments. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "How do you usually pay for things, and do you carry cash at all?",
    "What are the main benefits of digital payments for consumers and businesses?",
    "Some people worry that a cashless society would exclude certain groups. Is this concern justified?",
    "If cash disappeared completely, what would be lost, and what would be gained?"],
    m: [
      "I almost never use cash now. I pay with my phone in shops and with a card online, and I split restaurant bills through a payment app. I only keep a twenty-euro note in my wallet for emergencies, and it's been there for months. Sometimes I'm caught off guard, like at a village market last summer where only cash was accepted, and I had to walk fifteen minutes to find an ATM. It was a bit embarrassing, but also a good reminder that not everyone has made the same shift. For me, digital payment is simply faster and easier to track.",
      "For consumers, the main benefits are speed, convenience and security. You don't need to carry money, a lost card can be blocked immediately, and every transaction leaves a record, which helps with budgeting. For businesses, it reduces the costs and risks of handling cash, such as theft, counting errors and trips to the bank. It also speeds up queues, so more customers get served. Digital payments make accounting easier too, since the data is already recorded. The downside is the fees that merchants pay, which smaller businesses sometimes find burdensome, but overall the efficiency gains are considerable.",
      "I think it's justified, and it shouldn't be dismissed. Elderly people who aren't comfortable with technology, people without a bank account, and visitors from abroad could be put at a disadvantage. In rural areas, poor internet connections or breakdowns can leave people unable to buy basic goods. There's also the question of dependence on private payment companies and the vulnerability of the system to cyberattacks or power cuts. So, before moving further toward a cashless society, governments should guarantee that everyone has access to a basic account and that shops are legally required to accept cash, at least for essentials.",
      "What we would gain is efficiency and a reduction in some kinds of crime, like tax evasion and money laundering, since every payment would be traceable. What we would lose is anonymity, and that's not a trivial point. Cash allows people to make private purchases without leaving a record, and if every transaction is visible to banks or governments, there's potential for surveillance. It would also remove a safety net, since cash works when electronic systems fail. I think a society can function without cash, but it should do so with strong privacy protections, otherwise convenience could come at the expense of freedom."] },
  { id: "iz022", diff: 4, g: "M", topic: "Aging populations", ctx: "You are participating in a research project about generations and society. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "How do older people contribute to the community where you live?",
    "Many countries are facing an aging population. What do you think are the biggest challenges this creates?",
    "Some people argue that the retirement age should be raised. What is your view?",
    "What can younger and older generations learn from each other?"],
    m: [
      "In my neighborhood, older people hold things together more than they realize. A retired couple on my street runs a free homework club in the local library on Wednesday afternoons, and a former nurse volunteers at the community center, teaching first aid. My neighbor, who is in her eighties, looks after the building's shared garden and knows everyone's name. They also contribute economically, since many of them look after grandchildren, which allows the parents to work. I think society often sees older people as dependent, but in practice, many of them provide a significant amount of unpaid work and social glue.",
      "The most pressing one is financial: fewer working people are supporting a growing number of retirees, which puts pressure on pension systems and public budgets. Healthcare is another challenge, because older populations need more care, and there's already a shortage of nurses and caregivers in many countries. There's also a labor market issue, since a shrinking workforce can slow economic growth. And socially, loneliness among older people is a serious problem that doesn't get enough attention. Governments will need to combine several measures, such as encouraging later retirement, supporting immigration and investing in preventive health.",
      "I understand the arithmetic behind it, and I think some increase is probably unavoidable, since people live much longer than when pension systems were designed. But a single age for everyone is unfair, because not everyone has the same physical capacities. It's much harder for a construction worker or a nurse to work until sixty-eight than for an office employee. So I'd favor flexibility: gradual retirement, with reduced hours, and earlier exit options for physically demanding jobs. The reform will only be accepted if people feel that the burden is shared fairly, and that older workers aren't being pushed out by employers anyway.",
      "I think the exchange works in both directions. Older people can share experience, perspective and patience, which young people often lack. They've seen crises come and go, so they can help us put current problems in context. At work, mentoring from a senior colleague can save a young person years of trial and error. Younger people, in turn, can help older ones with technology, and they bring new ideas and different attitudes. In my case, teaching my grandfather to use video calls was a great experience for both of us. Programs that bring generations together, like shared housing, benefit everyone, and I'd like to see more of them."] },
  { id: "iz023", diff: 4, g: "W", topic: "Rules, laws and individual freedom", ctx: "You are taking part in a study about citizens and the law. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Is there a rule or regulation in your daily life that you find particularly sensible or particularly annoying?",
    "Why do you think some people tend to ignore rules when they believe nobody is watching?",
    "Some argue that governments should be allowed to restrict personal freedom in order to protect public health. Where do you stand?",
    "Do you think that laws should change as quickly as society does?"],
    m: [
      "One I find very sensible is the ban on smoking in indoor public places. I remember restaurants where the air was almost unbreathable, and it's hard to imagine going back to that. It protects employees, who can't just choose another job, and it also encouraged many smokers to cut down. On the other hand, I find some administrative rules rather absurd. For instance, to prove an address you sometimes need a document that you can only get if you already have a proof of address. It's the sort of circular logic that makes people lose patience with bureaucracy.",
      "I think it comes down to the difference between obeying out of fear and obeying out of conviction. If someone follows a rule only because they might be caught, then the moment the risk disappears, so does the compliance. People are more likely to respect a rule when they understand its purpose and believe it's fair. Social norms matter too: if everyone around you ignores a regulation, you feel foolish for following it. Psychological studies show that people usually cheat a little, as long as they can still see themselves as honest. So enforcement is useful, but the legitimacy of the rule matters even more.",
      "I think it can be justified, but only under strict conditions. The restriction should be proportionate to the danger, based on solid evidence, limited in time and subject to review by courts or parliament. During the pandemic, temporary measures such as quarantines were arguably reasonable, because one person's behavior could directly harm others. Where I'd be cautious is when the argument is used to control behavior that mainly affects the individual, like diet. The risk is a slippery slope in which every freedom can be limited in the name of safety. So the burden of proof must always lie with those who want to restrict freedom, not with those who want to keep it.",
      "Not necessarily, and probably not that quickly. Laws need a degree of stability so that individuals and businesses can plan, and changing them hastily often leads to poorly designed rules that cause new problems. On the other hand, when technology evolves faster than legislation, as with artificial intelligence or cryptocurrencies, there is a dangerous gap in which harmful practices continue unchecked. A reasonable approach might be to use flexible, principle-based regulation, with regular reviews, rather than detailed rules that become outdated within months. So laws should adapt, but through careful consultation, not by following every passing trend."] },
  { id: "iz024", diff: 4, g: "M", topic: "Saving and investing", ctx: "You are participating in a study about young adults and financial decisions. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Do you currently save any of your money? If so, how and for what purpose?",
    "Why do you think many young people hesitate to start investing?",
    "Some economists claim that people are naturally bad at saving for the long term. Why might that be the case?",
    "Should governments encourage ordinary citizens to invest in financial markets? Explain your reasoning."],
    m: [
      "Yes, I try to put aside about fifteen percent of whatever I earn, whether from my internship or from tutoring. I transfer it automatically on the day I'm paid, so I never see it in my current account, and that way I'm not tempted to spend it. Part of it goes into a savings account as an emergency fund, which I want to be equal to about three months of expenses. The rest is for a longer-term project: I'd like to finance part of my master's abroad without relying entirely on loans. It's slow, but seeing the balance grow each month is quite motivating.",
      "I think the main reasons are fear, lack of knowledge and a perceived lack of money. Many young people have heard stories about people losing everything in a crash, and they assume it's like gambling. Financial vocabulary can also be intimidating, and nobody teaches it at school. Then there's the feeling that investing is for the wealthy, so unless you have thousands to spare, it's not worth starting. In reality, with low-cost index funds, you can begin with very small amounts, and the earlier you start, the more time compounding has to work in your favor. A modest amount at twenty can outperform a larger one at forty.",
      "I think it's mainly due to present bias: we overvalue immediate rewards and undervalue future ones. A new phone today feels more real than a comfortable retirement in forty years. Behavioral economists have shown that people are consistently overconfident about their future self-control. There's also loss aversion, which makes people uncomfortable with market fluctuations even when the long-term trend is positive. And the benefits of saving are invisible, while the pleasure of spending is immediate. That's why systems like automatic enrollment in pension plans work so well: they harness inertia in our favor, rather than asking us to fight our impulses every month.",
      "I believe they should, with safeguards. Encouraging participation in markets can help households build wealth and reduce dependence on state pensions, which are under strain. Measures such as tax-advantaged accounts or automatic enrollment make a real difference. However, ordinary citizens are exposed to risks they may not fully understand, so governments must also guarantee financial education and strong consumer protection, to prevent mis-selling of complex products. Otherwise, you might encourage people to invest in things that harm them. Done properly, broader participation can also give citizens a stake in the economy, which isn't a bad thing for social cohesion."] },
  { id: "iz025", diff: 4, g: "W", topic: "Healthcare systems", ctx: "You have agreed to take part in a study about health and public services. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Have you had experience with the healthcare system in your country? How would you describe it?",
    "What do you think makes a healthcare system effective?",
    "Some argue that preventive care should receive much more funding than treatment. Do you agree?",
    "How might technology change the relationship between doctors and patients in the coming years?"],
    m: [
      "In general, my experience has been positive. When I broke my wrist playing football a few years ago, I was seen within an hour at the emergency department, and the follow-up appointments were easy to schedule. I paid very little out of pocket, because most of the cost was covered by public insurance. The only frustration is waiting for specialists. When my sister needed an appointment with a dermatologist, it took nearly three months. So I'd say the system is accessible and of good quality, but under pressure, particularly in terms of staff shortages and long waiting times.",
      "I think there are a few essential ingredients. First, access: people shouldn't avoid seeing a doctor because they can't afford it. Second, quality, meaning well-trained staff, modern equipment and clear protocols. Third, efficiency, because resources are limited, and money wasted on administration or unnecessary tests can't be used for patients. Coordination matters too: a patient should be able to move smoothly between the family doctor, the hospital and the pharmacy, without repeating the same information each time. Finally, I'd add trust. If patients trust their doctors and feel listened to, they follow treatments better, and that improves outcomes significantly.",
      "Broadly, yes. It's far cheaper to prevent a disease than to treat it, and the benefits are both human and economic. Vaccination campaigns, screening for cancer, and programs to reduce smoking or obesity have saved countless lives and large sums of money. The problem is that prevention is politically unattractive, since its results appear years later and are invisible: nobody celebrates a heart attack that didn't happen. Hospitals, by contrast, are visible and emotional. That said, I wouldn't cut treatment funding to pay for prevention, because people who are ill need care now. The right approach is to increase prevention without neglecting acute needs.",
      "I think technology will make the relationship more continuous and more collaborative. Wearable devices can monitor a patient's heart rate or glucose levels in real time, so doctors will be able to detect problems earlier and adjust treatment remotely. Consultations by video will save time, especially for people in remote regions. Artificial intelligence may help with diagnosis, for example by analyzing scans, which would free doctors to spend more time with patients. The risk is that the human dimension gets lost, or that sensitive data is misused. So I hope technology will support doctors rather than replace the empathy and judgment that patients need, particularly when the news is bad."] },
  { id: "iz026", diff: 4, g: "M", topic: "Patience and instant gratification", ctx: "You are participating in a study about habits and psychology in the digital age. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Are you a patient person? Can you give an example from your own life?",
    "In a world of instant messaging and same-day delivery, do you think people are becoming less patient?",
    "Some psychologists argue that the ability to delay gratification predicts success in life. What is your reaction to this claim?",
    "How can educators or parents help children develop patience?"],
    m: [
      "I'd say I'm patient with people but not with systems. If a friend is late, I don't mind, but if a website takes more than a few seconds to load, I get irritated. The most patient I've been was probably when I taught myself to play the guitar. For the first three months, my fingers hurt and everything sounded terrible. I almost quit twice. But I kept practicing for twenty minutes every evening, and gradually, the chords started to sound like music. That experience taught me that some rewards can't be rushed, and that frustration is part of the process.",
      "I think so, yes, at least in terms of expectations. If you've become used to receiving a reply in seconds and a parcel the next day, a delay of even a few hours starts to feel like a failure of the system. Some studies suggest that our attention spans for online content have shortened, and people abandon videos or pages that don't capture them immediately. That said, I wouldn't say people have become fundamentally impatient. We still wait years for a degree or a career. It's rather that we've been trained to expect speed in some domains, and this habit spills over into others where speed isn't possible.",
      "I'd treat it with caution. The famous marshmallow experiment did show a correlation between the ability to wait and later outcomes. But later research suggested that the link is much weaker once you take into account family background. A child from a stable, well-off home has good reason to wait, because promises are usually kept, whereas a child from an unpredictable environment may rationally take the immediate reward. So it's not simply a matter of willpower. That doesn't mean self-control is irrelevant, but it does mean that circumstances shape behavior more than the original interpretation suggested.",
      "I think the best way is to practice it in small, concrete situations. Parents can involve children in activities that require waiting, such as growing a plant or baking a cake, so they see that patience leads to a reward. It also helps to be reliable: if you promise something later, you must deliver, otherwise the child learns that waiting is pointless. Teachers can assign long-term projects broken into stages, with feedback along the way. And adults should model patience themselves, since children observe how we react to queues and delays. It's a skill built slowly, through repeated experiences, not through lectures."] },
  { id: "iz027", diff: 4, g: "W", topic: "Globalization and local identity", ctx: "You have agreed to take part in a study about global culture and local life. A researcher will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Are there any global brands or products that have become part of your everyday life?",
    "Do you think globalization has made the world more similar or more diverse? Why?",
    "Some people argue that small local businesses cannot survive in a globalized economy. Is this true?",
    "How can communities preserve their cultural identity while remaining open to the rest of the world?"],
    m: [
      "Quite a few, to be honest. My phone and laptop are made by American companies, and manufactured in Asia. I watch series produced in the United States and South Korea, and my shoes are from a global sportswear brand. Even my morning coffee often comes from a multinational chain. It's easy not to notice this until you try to avoid it. I once spent a week trying to buy only local products, and I found it surprisingly difficult, particularly for electronics. It made me realize how deeply embedded international supply chains are in the most ordinary routines.",
      "I'd say both, which sounds contradictory, but isn't. On the surface, cities around the world look increasingly alike, with the same shops, the same fast-food chains and the same pop songs. That's the homogenizing effect. Yet at the same time, globalization lets people access an enormous range of cultures that were previously out of reach: I can eat Ethiopian food, listen to Brazilian music and follow Indian cinema from my sofa. So differences between countries may be shrinking, while the variety available to each individual is growing. It's a shift from diversity between places to diversity within them.",
      "I think that's overly pessimistic. Large companies have enormous advantages in price and scale, and many traditional shops have disappeared. But small businesses can compete in other ways: quality, personal service, authenticity. A bakery that uses local ingredients and knows its customers can offer something a supermarket can't. The internet has also helped small producers reach customers worldwide, for example artisans selling online. They survive best when they specialize in a niche rather than competing on price. Of course, local authorities can help through supportive policies, like lower business taxes or priority for local suppliers in public contracts.",
      "I think identity is strongest when it's lived, not merely protected. If a language, a craft or a festival is only preserved in a museum, it eventually becomes folklore. The communities that keep their identity are usually those where younger people still find the traditions meaningful, and where they are free to reinterpret them. Openness doesn't threaten that, provided it is a two-way exchange: people can adopt foreign influences and also export their own culture. Education matters, of course, as does public support for local languages and arts. But above all, the culture has to remain useful and enjoyable, not just a duty toward the past."] },
  { id: "iz028", diff: 4, g: "M", topic: "Making major decisions", ctx: "You are participating in a study about how people make important choices. An interviewer will ask you four questions.", q: [
    "Tell me about an important decision you made recently. How did you reach it?",
    "When facing a difficult decision, do you rely more on logic or on intuition?",
    "Some people think having too many options makes decisions harder and people less satisfied. Do you agree?",
    "To what extent should we consider the long-term consequences of a decision, given that the future is so uncertain?"],
    m: [
      "The most important recent decision was applying to master's programs abroad rather than staying in my home country. I spent about two months thinking about it. I made a list of pros and cons, and I spoke to three people who had studied abroad, as well as a professor who knew my situation. The financial side worried me most, because it meant borrowing money. What finally tipped the balance was imagining myself in five years: I realized I would regret not trying much more than I would regret a temporary financial burden. So I decided to apply, and I'm very glad I did.",
      "I try to use both, in sequence. First, logic: I gather information, compare options and think through the consequences. But there often comes a point at which the analysis doesn't give a clear winner, and in that case I pay attention to my gut feeling. If I notice a sense of relief when I imagine choosing one option, or a sense of dread with another, I take that seriously. Intuition isn't magic; it's accumulated experience in a compressed form. However, in finance, I've learned to be wary of intuition, because it is easily affected by biases like overconfidence, so I always test it against data.",
      "Broadly, yes, and there's psychological research to support it. Barry Schwartz called this the paradox of choice: when you're faced with dozens of options, you spend more time comparing, you worry about missing a better one, and you feel less satisfied with whatever you choose. I've experienced it when choosing a streaming series or a restaurant: I spend forty minutes browsing and then I'm not even hungry. That said, some choice is clearly good, since having no options is worse. The sweet spot is probably a manageable set, perhaps three to five, with a clear criterion for choosing.",
      "I'd say we should consider them seriously, but without expecting certainty. We can't predict the future, but we can think about scenarios and ask which decision keeps the most options open. In finance, for example, diversification is precisely a response to uncertainty: you don't know which investment will do well, so you avoid putting everything in one place. The same logic applies to careers. Choose paths that build transferable skills and reversible commitments. What I'd avoid is paralysis, because refusing to decide is itself a decision, and usually not the best one. So: think ahead, remain flexible and accept that you'll sometimes be wrong."] }
);
})();
